1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,240
This video contains a
few additional tips

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:05,865
and hints for implementing
assignment 5.

3
00:00:05,865 --> 00:00:09,570
First, if you haven't been
using this previously,

4
00:00:09,570 --> 00:00:12,315
I suggest turning
on Wall and Werror.

5
00:00:12,315 --> 00:00:13,620
This is something that's covered

6
00:00:13,620 --> 00:00:15,255
in department coding guidelines,

7
00:00:15,255 --> 00:00:17,235
but it really helps in

8
00:00:17,235 --> 00:00:19,875
finding problems in
your source code.

9
00:00:19,875 --> 00:00:21,960
There's an example here of

10
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:28,170
using a segfaulter
implementation

11
00:00:28,170 --> 00:00:33,330
to demonstrate the benefit
of using warnings with

12
00:00:33,330 --> 00:00:35,610
your compiler and
treating warnings

13
00:00:35,610 --> 00:00:38,475
as errors for some
common warnings.

14
00:00:38,475 --> 00:00:42,205
I've got a main program
here that calls segfaulter,

15
00:00:42,205 --> 00:00:45,140
which is a function located
in a different file,

16
00:00:45,140 --> 00:00:47,900
and you can see here
the segfaulter function

17
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:48,980
itself expects

18
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:50,720
you to pass some
string in and then

19
00:00:50,720 --> 00:00:52,910
it's just going to calculate
the length of the string.

20
00:00:52,910 --> 00:00:55,485
In the main application,

21
00:00:55,485 --> 00:00:57,380
there's a bug here in
that we're not passing

22
00:00:57,380 --> 00:01:00,365
any string in as a part of
the call to segfaulter.

23
00:01:00,365 --> 00:01:04,880
If I compile and run this
without warnings and errors,

24
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:06,500
I'll get a segmentation fault,

25
00:01:06,500 --> 00:01:08,210
and the reason I get
a segmentation fault

26
00:01:08,210 --> 00:01:09,980
is because I'm not
passing anything

27
00:01:09,980 --> 00:01:11,900
in to this somestring and if I

28
00:01:11,900 --> 00:01:14,000
attempt to access this variable,

29
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:15,650
it's just going to pull
something randomly off

30
00:01:15,650 --> 00:01:17,825
the heap and most likely crash.

31
00:01:17,825 --> 00:01:24,040
This could cause a confusing
bug with your program.

32
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:27,860
Alternatively, if
I do include in

33
00:01:27,860 --> 00:01:30,170
my C flags Wall and Werror,

34
00:01:30,170 --> 00:01:32,810
I get a message like this
telling me that there

35
00:01:32,810 --> 00:01:34,280
wasn't a declaration of

36
00:01:34,280 --> 00:01:35,890
the function before
I tried to call it.

37
00:01:35,890 --> 00:01:38,495
So this is giving me
the hint that I needed

38
00:01:38,495 --> 00:01:42,405
to supply the declaration
of the function,

39
00:01:42,405 --> 00:01:43,675
and then I need to supply

40
00:01:43,675 --> 00:01:45,200
the right parameters in

41
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:47,850
order to avoid a
segmentation fault.

42
00:01:48,530 --> 00:01:53,205
A few other things to think
about with assignment 5.

43
00:01:53,205 --> 00:01:56,150
You want to close all
the file descriptors,

44
00:01:56,150 --> 00:01:57,770
both from the socket and the

45
00:01:57,770 --> 00:02:02,370
accept methods to
avoid memory leaks.

46
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:05,240
There are different
ways to implement

47
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:08,705
your signal handler
strategy for shutdown,

48
00:02:08,705 --> 00:02:10,790
but I think the shutdown command

49
00:02:10,790 --> 00:02:14,250
is one that might
particularly be useful.

50
00:02:14,250 --> 00:02:16,640
There's some links here at
the bottom to check out,

51
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:19,580
but this is one that I
suggest taking a look at if

52
00:02:19,580 --> 00:02:20,780
you're trying to think of how to

53
00:02:20,780 --> 00:02:23,185
implement your signal
handler strategy.

54
00:02:23,185 --> 00:02:26,685
In terms of bind failing with
'Address Already in Use',

55
00:02:26,685 --> 00:02:28,130
this could happen
sometimes if you

56
00:02:28,130 --> 00:02:29,720
close your application
and immediately

57
00:02:29,720 --> 00:02:33,005
restart it so the
SO_REUSEADDR option

58
00:02:33,005 --> 00:02:35,780
on bind is one way
that you can make

59
00:02:35,780 --> 00:02:39,345
this less likely to occur and

60
00:02:39,345 --> 00:02:41,300
there's some links
again here on the slide

61
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:43,945
that you can look at for
more detail about that.

62
00:02:43,945 --> 00:02:46,010
One other thing I wanted to talk

63
00:02:46,010 --> 00:02:47,540
about with respect to Assignment

64
00:02:47,540 --> 00:02:49,355
5 is to help

65
00:02:49,355 --> 00:02:51,470
ensure that you don't have
memory leaks in your program,

66
00:02:51,470 --> 00:02:54,035
you can use an application
called Valgrind.

67
00:02:54,035 --> 00:02:56,795
This is a really super-useful
application that

68
00:02:56,795 --> 00:02:59,240
essentially is a wrapper around

69
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:01,490
your executable to look at

70
00:03:01,490 --> 00:03:04,970
the memory allocations you do
and show that you've freed

71
00:03:04,970 --> 00:03:08,130
all the memory that you've
allocated and the way

72
00:03:08,130 --> 00:03:11,325
we'd run this with our
aesdsocket application,

73
00:03:11,325 --> 00:03:14,055
we could say valgrind
leak check equal full,

74
00:03:14,055 --> 00:03:15,920
show leak kinds
all, track origins,

75
00:03:15,920 --> 00:03:18,680
yes, verbose, and then
you can specify dash,

76
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:21,170
dash log file to
output to a log file,

77
00:03:21,170 --> 00:03:22,580
and then just run your

78
00:03:22,580 --> 00:03:24,680
aesdsocket application
like you would

79
00:03:24,680 --> 00:03:29,580
herewith to run
it interactively.

80
00:03:29,630 --> 00:03:33,050
What you should see
from that output file,

81
00:03:33,050 --> 00:03:34,730
you should see an output message

82
00:03:34,730 --> 00:03:36,350
here saying all heap
blocks were freed,

83
00:03:36,350 --> 00:03:37,595
and no leaks are possible.

84
00:03:37,595 --> 00:03:39,725
So try this out

85
00:03:39,725 --> 00:03:41,480
and see if this is working

86
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:43,295
with your assignment
5 implementation.

87
00:03:43,295 --> 00:03:44,540
This will be a part of the unit

88
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:45,680
test to the assignment 6,

89
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:47,870
so implementing
assignment 5 without

90
00:03:47,870 --> 00:03:51,630
memory leaks will help
you with assignment 6.